      "raw_payload": {
        "id": "R1",
        "kind": "hypothesis",
        "claim": "Speculatively, a provenance ledger could recognize an already represented retroactive correction across changed row identifiers, preventing a second application while preserving every reported occurrence.",
        "delta": "Change the reconciliation dependency from row-ID replacement alone to an evidence graph carried by immutable row occurrences, explicit supersession edges, status meanings and cutoff eligibility. Matching normalized quantities suggest candidate aliases but never establish identity. Nearest rival: literal superseded-row lookup with the same occurrence-preserving ledger.",
        "prediction": "If explicit records uniquely establish cross-report representation, the graph should reconcile the awkward amendment without double application. The rival should retain a disagreement when literal identifiers cannot establish representation. Removing a needed supersession edge or introducing an undefined status should withdraw affected definite conclusions. If both variants behave identically, the proposed capability remains unconfirmed.",
        "probe": "Within the sixty-minute review, use the local script or spreadsheet to run both variants on both supplied copies, reserving ten minutes to freeze twelve per-batch outputs and record citations. Retain duplicate and unmatched occurrences; normalize only U=5V; never impute neighboring quantities. Each output is a quantity or an explicit disagreement describing competing records and unknowns. Issue at most three prioritized requests, each naming a missing field or record; preserve remaining disagreements. During the separate twenty-minute comparison, open the sealed key and compare resolved-batch counts, unsupported definite claims and request counts. Examine whether the disturbed records specifically change justified conclusions. No distinguishing case makes the experiment inconclusive.",
        "fit": "uncertain",
        "assumptions": [
          "The supplied records may lack authoritative links between differently identified replacement rows; quantity equality and chronology alone cannot supply them.",
          "Defeat conditions: a claimed alias lacks explicit support, a definite quantity survives loss of indispensable evidence, or the sealed key contradicts it.",
          "Capacity is unproved: if constructing and checking both variants cannot fit the review budget, freeze bounded disagreements. More than three independent gaps limits follow-up coverage without permitting invented resolution."
        ],
